.TH ZIG_STDLIB 7 "2026-10-06" "Zig 0.17.0" "Zig Manual"
.SH NAME
zig-stdlib \- overview of the Zig Standard Library
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Zig Standard Library contains commonly used algorithms, data
structures, and definitions to help build programs or libraries.
.PP
The library ships with every Zig distribution. It is not a separate
package, and no external dependency is required to use it.
.PP
The Zig Standard Library is documented separately from the language
reference. The upstream documentation is available at:
.PP
.RS 4
\fBhttps://ziglang.org/documentation/master/std/\fR
.RE
.SH LOCAL DOCUMENTATION
A local, offline copy of the Standard Library documentation is provided
with each Zig distribution. It can be served over a local web server:
.nf
    zig std
.fi
.PP
This command starts a small HTTP server on a local port and prints the
URL to standard output. Open the URL in a web browser to read the
documentation. Press Ctrl-C to stop the server.
.PP
The documentation is generated from doc comments in the source. It
reflects the exact version of the Standard Library that is installed
with the Zig compiler being used.
.SH USE IN THIS MANUAL
Many pages in this manual reference types, functions, and modules from
the Standard Library. Refer to the upstream documentation for the
complete API. This page only provides an overview; it does not document
individual functions.
.PP
Commonly referenced modules:
.TP
\fBstd\.mem\fR
Allocators, slices, and memory utilities.
.TP
\fBstd\.fs\fR
Filesystem operations: file handles, directories, paths.
.TP
\fBstd\.io\fR
Input and output primitives.
.TP
\fBstd\.fmt\fR
Formatted printing and parsing.
.TP
\fBstd\.math\fR
Numeric functions and constants.
.TP
\fBstd\.testing\fR
Assertions and test utilities.
.TP
\fBstd\.json\fR
JSON serialization and deserialization.
.TP
\fBstd\.process\fR
Process arguments, environment, and spawning.
.TP
\fBstd\.Thread\fR
Threads, mutexes, and synchronization primitives.
.TP
\fBstd\.heap\fR
General-purpose allocators: \fBGPA\fR, \fBc_allocator\fR,
\fBpage_allocator\fR.
.SH ALLOCATOR MODEL
The Standard Library does not have a default allocator. Any function
that allocates memory takes an allocator as a parameter. This makes the
library usable in freestanding environments, operating system kernels,
and embedded targets where no default heap exists.
.PP
See \fBstd\.mem\.Allocator\fR for the allocator interface.
